A||||8||1988LRC||oil level question||||5||
Z||000000||1988LRC||04-20-2001||08:19 AM||||after an oil & filter change on my 88 2m4 the level on the dipstick is just at the add mark at the bottom of the crosshatching, the owners manual and the Haynes list capacity as 4 qts. I used exactly that amount has anyone else experienced this and should I add the extra qt. to bring the level to the full mark? TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||04-20-2001||09:33 AM||||Make sure you wait long enough for all the oil to get down there, but if it is a quart low, add a quart and check it again to make sure that the level is correct.<P>What filter are you using?  The manual says a PF51, but everybody now uses PF52 becuase of the extra capacity (almost double of PF51 I believe)<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Fiero87311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto near completion! :D<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||04-20-2001||09:59 AM||||You must have a replacement dipstick as they do indeed take 4 quarts to fill (all 2.5's)irreguardless of the filter, it's in the pan of '88 4 bangers.||||reg||1
Z||000003||1988LRC||04-20-2001||10:12 AM||||it was a 1 owner car when I got it and the stick is original as far as I can tell, was curious if anyone else had had this situation||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||04-20-2001||10:17 AM||||Add oil until the level is in the middle of the crosshatch pattern. That's where you want it to be.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||SteveJ||04-20-2001||10:41 AM||||Oil dip sticks are a crude but effective method of determining how much oil is in there. Look up how much oil is supposed to be in there and put in that much and run the car. Then with the engine cold see where it comes on your particular dip stick. That's the full mark.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fierowrecker||04-20-2001||06:32 PM||||Running a quart low is no problem, as long as YOU know you have 4 quarts in there...<BR>But running a quart too much (5)could cause foaming due to the crank slamming into the oil...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Rrazzz||04-20-2001||09:15 PM||||I've had similar problem, but worse. My 1988 4 cyl takes 4.2 quarts to bring the oil level to bottom of the cross hatched area on the dip stick. Never determined the proper capacity. So, I split the difference and have been using 4.5 to 4.7 quarts. Wonder if it could be an incorrect dip stick tube, or tube not fully inserted into block?||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||04-21-2001||07:50 AM||||MY V6 takes 4.5 qts with oil filter change. I also have a little extra oil line to fill that runs to my turbo bearing.||||reg||1
